WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.559
We can all learn, doesn't matter
what our age is, doesn't matter

00:00:03.560 --> 00:00:06.110
what our presumed talent is.

00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:07.390
We're constantly learning.

00:00:07.400 --> 00:00:10.869
And the reason I raise this subject is
because someone commented here on my

00:00:10.870 --> 00:00:15.310
YouTube channel, as an older person,
you have to learn like a stroke victim.

00:00:15.339 --> 00:00:17.659
You have to use different
parts of the brain, et cetera.

00:00:18.809 --> 00:00:19.199
Okay.

00:00:19.370 --> 00:00:23.390
Older people, we now know they
retain their neuroplasticity.

00:00:23.550 --> 00:00:27.310
Anytime you're learning something, you
are creating new pathways in the brain.

00:00:27.310 --> 00:00:28.699
You are renewing your brain.

00:00:29.085 --> 00:00:32.384
Today, for example, I've been
trying to set up my studio here.

00:00:32.515 --> 00:00:33.665
It was a lot of work.

00:00:33.665 --> 00:00:35.575
I had to learn a lot of new things.

00:00:35.635 --> 00:00:40.365
And I'm not naturally patient when it
comes to doing things with my hands.

00:00:40.455 --> 00:00:45.124
But I realize I have to be patient if
I'm going to set all these things up.

00:00:45.335 --> 00:00:48.204
Three different sets of acoustic panels.

00:00:48.344 --> 00:00:50.024
These sconces you see behind me.

00:00:50.515 --> 00:00:54.265
Lots of work, but all the
while I'm conscious of forging

00:00:54.265 --> 00:00:55.855
new habits in my brain.

00:00:55.855 --> 00:00:57.235
That's what learning is all about.

00:00:57.495 --> 00:00:59.325
And languages are the same way.

00:00:59.465 --> 00:01:03.434
So it doesn't matter if as an older
person, it's harder for me, or maybe

00:01:03.435 --> 00:01:07.794
it isn't, or maybe it is, or because
I have a lot of practice at learning.

00:01:07.794 --> 00:01:09.014
I'm used to doing it.

00:01:09.355 --> 00:01:13.895
It doesn't matter in learning a
language, we are enabling our brain

00:01:13.895 --> 00:01:16.604
to form a model of another language.

00:01:16.725 --> 00:01:21.144
We form that model through
repetitive exposure to the language.

00:01:21.335 --> 00:01:24.784
Eventually we start to be able to
predict what's going to come at us,

00:01:24.785 --> 00:01:26.504
which helps us in our comprehension.

00:01:26.875 --> 00:01:30.765
It also helps us when we want to
express ourselves, we want to produce

00:01:30.765 --> 00:01:36.045
an utterance based on the accumulated
experience that we have with the language.

00:01:36.174 --> 00:01:40.354
But anything you're learning is
constantly forging new connections

00:01:40.585 --> 00:01:42.975
in your brain, new synapses.

00:01:43.235 --> 00:01:44.255
And so the idea that...

00:01:44.910 --> 00:01:49.999
Some people don't have the ability that
their brains won't learn new things.

00:01:50.249 --> 00:01:54.850
If I want to do it, if I stay with
it, if I continue, I will learn.

00:01:55.049 --> 00:01:59.749
So the issue becomes more, how do
you encourage people to stay with it?

00:01:59.919 --> 00:02:04.720
Well, one of the things is to make sure
people understand that they can learn.

00:02:04.910 --> 00:02:07.550
The other is anything that
can nudge people forward.

00:02:07.804 --> 00:02:11.494
So in my case, I went and
bought a, a little hand drill.

00:02:11.774 --> 00:02:14.695
Well, now I got to use the hand drill
because I've got to drill the holes

00:02:14.695 --> 00:02:17.374
to mount my acoustic pads on the wall.

00:02:17.635 --> 00:02:18.964
So that kind of nudges me.

00:02:18.964 --> 00:02:20.774
I can't give up on the project.

00:02:20.774 --> 00:02:21.954
I've got to stay with it.

00:02:22.144 --> 00:02:25.834
Or even, you know, something like in
the morning, I think there's a certain

00:02:25.844 --> 00:02:32.145
discipline in doing my seven minute
exercise muscle booster, but sometimes I

00:02:32.145 --> 00:02:36.114
don't really feel like doing it, but then
I'm nudged forward by saying I will go.

00:02:36.540 --> 00:02:44.239
To, uh, Radio Fardo, download some Farsi,
the news of the day, and, and then I

00:02:44.239 --> 00:02:46.480
import it into LingQ, the sound file.

00:02:46.480 --> 00:02:50.549
So I get a transcript and the fact
that I'm going to be grabbing this

00:02:50.549 --> 00:02:55.899
sound file from Radio Fardo nudges
me to do my morning exercises.

00:02:56.300 --> 00:02:58.390
So there's a lot of
little things like that.

00:02:58.400 --> 00:03:03.510
A person you met, a class, for
example, but we have to remember that

00:03:03.540 --> 00:03:07.120
the way we're going to progress in
the language is if we can continue

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:09.009
to expose ourself to the language.

00:03:09.090 --> 00:03:11.350
It almost doesn't matter
what you're able to produce.

00:03:11.519 --> 00:03:15.189
It almost doesn't matter what you're able
to say and how many mistakes you make.

00:03:15.390 --> 00:03:17.060
What matters is that you continue.

00:03:17.570 --> 00:03:22.790
Forging these new pathways in your
brain, as I did today, putting all

00:03:22.790 --> 00:03:24.650
these acoustic panels up on the wall.

00:03:24.840 --> 00:03:28.870
It's not that I can hire myself out
as a handyman, but I feel that I

00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:32.039
created some tolerance for frustration.

00:03:32.109 --> 00:03:37.059
And so consistency, perseverance,
a few things that not just forward.

00:03:37.320 --> 00:03:40.410
As long as we keep going,
anyone can learn, regardless

00:03:40.590 --> 00:03:43.600
of age or presumed aptitude.

00:03:43.880 --> 00:03:47.660
Before I leave you, I want to leave a
couple of videos for you to look at.

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:51.699
One on whether there is a talent for
language learning, and another on

00:03:51.699 --> 00:03:56.530
whether age matters, or to what extent
age affects our ability to learn.

00:03:56.609 --> 00:03:59.859
I think both of these videos are
relevant, so please have a look.

